// Population: the two axes the same people are counted along, and the one they are not.
//
// `class` is the rung of the ladder - lower, middle, upper - and `sector` is what a trade
// lives off. The heading used to read SOCIAL CLASSES over a list that was secretly three
// different axes at once; it still reads that way here, because in English it is now true.
//
// POLITICS is the third question about the same people: not who they are but what they
// believe. It is the same list of ideologies the government itself is chosen from, read
// region by region from that region's own conditions, its laws and its form of government.
{
  "PEOPLES": "PEOPLES",
  "FAITHS": "FAITHS",
  "TRADES": "TRADES",
  "POLITICS": "POLITICS",
  "SOCIAL CLASSES": "SOCIAL CLASSES",
  "SECTORS": "SECTORS",

  "Lower class": "Lower class",
  "Middle class": "Middle class",
  "Upper class": "Upper class",

  "Services": "Services",
  "Ownership": "Ownership",

  "{0} people lean this way": "{0} people lean this way",
  "The position the government itself holds. What it holds here is the mandate it governs this region with.": "The position the government itself holds. What it holds here is the mandate it governs this region with.",
  "An opposition position. A region whose largest leaning is not the government's is a region nobody in the capital is speaking for.": "An opposition position. A region whose largest leaning is not the government's is a region nobody in the capital is speaking for.",

  "Everyone the country has, by how well off they are": "Everyone the country has, by how well off they are"
}
